<div class="book-title">Likutay Halachos — Orach Chaim</div>
<div class="halacha-title">Hilchos Betzi'as HaPas — Halachah 5<br><span style="font-size:1rem;font-weight:400;">Part 1: §§ 1–13</span></div>
<div class="lm-subtitle">Based on <span class="v">"L'Hamshich Shalom"</span> <span class="sr">(<span class="lr">LM I, 14</span>)</span></div>

<div class="halacha-intro">
<b>Halachic framework:</b> One must bless <i>HaMotzi</i> on a whole loaf. The blessing must conclude together with the bread [i.e. one must hold the bread while completing the <i>berachah</i>]. The Sages always called the one blessing <i>HaMotzi</i> the "<i>botze'a</i>" [breaker], not simply "the one who blesses." The <i>Shulchan Aruch</i> called these laws "<i>Hilchos Betzi'as HaPas</i>" — Laws of Breaking Bread. Similarly, all eating or giving bread to the poor is described in terms of breaking/splitting: <span class="v">"is it not to break your bread for the hungry?"</span> The Sages deliberated at length from where on the loaf one should break it, concluding: from where the baking began (<span class="h">mei'haicha d'kadim bishula</span>), or from where it completed.
</div>

<!-- §1 -->
<div class="sn">§ 1 — The Whole Loaf: <i>Yirah</i> and <i>Shalom</i></div>

<div class="p">The essential principle from <span class="lr">LM I:14</span>: one must complete the <span class="h">yirah</span> [fear/awe of Hashem] through the <span class="h">kavod</span> [glory] that is raised through converts and <span class="h">ba'alei teshuvah</span>, who are made through <span class="h">Toras Chesed</span> [Torah taught for the sake of teaching others]. When one merits <span class="h">yirah</span>, one merits <span class="h">shalom</span> — peace = completeness (<span class="h">sheleimus</span>). <span class="mx">"Where there is fear, there completeness is found."</span> Through this one merits prayer: <span class="v">"a woman who fears Hashem — she shall be praised."</span></div>

<!-- §2 -->
<div class="sn">§ 2 — The Whole Loaf = <i>Yirah</i> = Completeness</div>

<div class="p">When one must bless <span class="h">HaMotzi</span> and break the bread, one must seek a <span class="kc">whole loaf</span> (<span class="h">shalem</span>). The whole = <span class="h">yirah</span> = the root of completeness. The essential <span class="h">yirah</span> approaches a person <span class="kc">during eating</span> (as explained in <span class="lr">LM II:7</span>). The <span class="h">berachah</span> itself is through <span class="h">yirah</span>: only through <span class="h">yirah</span> can one truly praise Hashem — <span class="v">"a woman who fears Hashem — she shall be praised."</span></div>

<div class="p">The essential <span class="h">tikkun</span> of eating = drawing <span class="h">shalom</span> by completing <span class="h">yirah</span> through raising <span class="h">kavod</span> via making <span class="h">ba'alei teshuvah</span> and converts. Eating extracts holy sparks from food. These sparks = souls that fell and became mixed. Raising them through proper eating with a <span class="h">berachah</span> and heartfelt intention = making <span class="h">ba'alei teshuvah</span> and converts — for converts and <span class="h">ba'alei teshuvah</span> are also sparks and souls raised from where they fell. Through holy eating, Hashem's <span class="h">kavod</span> rises (as in <span class="lr">LM I:67</span>): <span class="mx">"the table of kings — the King of Glory."</span> Therefore <span class="h">yirah</span> comes during eating — because the <span class="h">yirah</span> is completed through the rise of <span class="h">kavod</span> from sparks extracted through eating. Therefore: seek a <span class="kc">whole loaf</span> = <span class="h">yirah</span> = completeness.</div>

<!-- §3 -->
<div class="sn">§ 3 — The Mystery of Breaking: <i>Birur</i> after <i>Birur</i></div>

<div class="p">Every thing in the world must be clarified (<span class="h">birur</span>) through <span class="kc">clarification after clarification</span> until reaching its ultimate completeness. This is seen most clearly in eating: the food arrives through many clarifications — first plowing and sowing, then reaping, then separating wheat from chaff and straw, then grinding and sifting from bran, then kneading into dough, dividing, baking. <span class="kc">At every stage, what seemed complete before is incomplete relative to the next stage.</span></div>

<div class="p">All this is because the original fall of the sparks was through the sin of eating — Adam's eating from the Tree of Knowledge. Therefore food requires the most clarifications: <span class="v">"by the sweat of your brow you shall eat bread."</span> Before each clarification, the thing is in the aspect of <span class="h">palga</span> [half/incomplete] — because of the <span class="kc">soiled garments</span> (<span class="h">begadim tzo'im</span>) still clinging to it. As explained in <span class="lr">LM I:14</span> on the teaching of Rabbah bar bar Chanah: <span class="mx">"at the meeting-point of the head, it cast a <i>palga</i> and blocked the Yarden"</span> — souls still far from holiness are called <span class="h">palga</span> because they are still clothed in soiled garments that block them like a river. <span class="v">"Remove the soiled garments!"</span> — only by stripping these garments can the obstructions be removed.</div>

<div class="p">Even when bread is whole and fit for the blessing — it still lacks its <span class="kc">ultimate completeness</span>. The final clarification occurs during eating itself: within the body, the food is ground, refined, and purified until the finest essence rises to sustain life, and the waste becomes literal refuse. <span class="kc">This is the ultimate stripping of the soiled garments</span> — and it only happens inside the person.</div>

<div class="p">Therefore the Sages were so careful about the breaking of the bread: one blesses on the <span class="kc">whole</span> (= <span class="h">yirah</span> = completeness of the previous clarification), then immediately <span class="kc">breaks</span> it (= beginning the ultimate clarification through eating). Through the breaking, one must intend to raise the bread from <span class="h">palga</span> to ultimate <span class="h">sheleimus</span> — stripping the good from the soiled garments completely, so that the life-force rises pure and the waste is expelled.</div>

<!-- §4 -->
<div class="sn">§ 4 — Rav Ashi and Menasheh: "Where Do You Break <i>HaMotzi</i>?"</div>

<div class="p">The Gemara (<span class="h">Sanhedrin</span> 102b): Rav Ashi said, "Tomorrow we will begin with our colleague [Menasheh, discussing his sins]." Menasheh appeared in a dream and asked: <span class="mx">"From where do you break <span class="h">HaMotzi</span>?"</span> Rav Ashi could not answer.</div>

<div class="p">Why did Menasheh choose precisely this question? Because <span class="h">avodah zarah</span> [idolatry] is called <span class="v">"vomit and filth"</span>: <span class="v">"all their tables are full of vomit and filth."</span> Its power to deceive comes from the soiled garments — which confuse the mind to the point of insanity, until one can reach idolatry: <span class="mx">"today it says 'do this,' tomorrow 'do that,' until…"</span></div>

<div class="p">Menasheh said: "You mock me for worshipping idolatry = soiled garments. If you are clean of them, tell me: <span class="kc">where do you break <span class="h">HaMotzi</span>?</span>" — meaning, do you know the secret of the breaking through which the food is clarified from the soiled garments? The essential purification from literal filth happens through eating. If you cannot answer this, you are not fully purified either. The only reason you didn't reach actual idolatry is that in your generation, the <span class="h">yetzer hara</span> for idolatry had already been slaughtered — not because you are cleaner.</div>

<!-- §5 -->
<div class="sn">§ 5 — Encouragement: The Soiled Garments Intensify Before the End</div>

<div class="p">Everyone approaching Hashem must know: the obstacles and barriers spread out enormously. Especially after one has already begun and advanced — sometimes after years of purification, one falls back and desires and evil thoughts attack with terrifying force. <span class="kc">All this comes from the soiled garments not yet fully stripped.</span> As explained in <span class="lr">LM I:14</span>: the soiled garments obstruct like a river that cannot be crossed. One needs tremendous effort. Because stripping the soiled garments requires such exertion, not everyone endures — and from this comes everything that befalls a person.</div>

<div class="p">This is understood through bread: even after the wheat has been purified through many clarifications until it is a finished loaf, <span class="kc">relative to the final clarification through eating, it has not yet begun its purification</span>. The ultimate separation from literal filth only happens inside the body. The earlier clarifications — however laborious — are nothing compared to the final one. Similarly: even someone who has purified himself significantly may face barriers more intense than at the beginning, because now the soiled garments themselves are being confronted directly. <span class="mx">"Here is the table, here is the meat… but we have no mouth to eat"</span> (<span class="h">Kiddushin</span> 46a).</div>

<!-- §6 -->
<div class="sn">§ 6 — Torah = Eating: Dovid's Prayer</div>

<div class="p">In particular and in general, every person must undergo this: clarification after clarification, with the final clarification (= eating) being hardest. Dovid prayed: <span class="v">"to do Your will, my G-d, I desire — and Your Torah is within my innards."</span> That the clarification through Torah and <span class="h">mitzvos</span> should be like the clarification of eating inside the body — where all waste is expelled completely. Therefore receiving and fulfilling Torah is called "eating": <span class="v">"eat this scroll"</span> (Yechezkel 3). The future promise: <span class="mx">"to eat to satisfaction and for garment of the Ancient One"</span> — eating without any grip of soiled garments, with pristine garments: <span class="v">"the Ancient of Days sat, His garment white as snow."</span> Yaakov requested: <span class="v">"bread to eat and a garment to wear"</span> — that eating should be in the aspect of clean garments. <span class="mx">"The Torah was only given to eaters of the <span class="h">man</span>"</span> — the <span class="h">man</span> was absorbed into the limbs without waste, food of angels, with no grip of soiled garments = Adam's food before the sin.</div>

<!-- §7 -->
<div class="sn">§ 7 — The Final Generation: The Hardest Clarification</div>

<div class="p">In the collective: the immense difficulty now for anyone wanting to serve Hashem, the bitterness of this exile in <span class="h">ikvesa d'Meshicha</span> — all because <span class="kc">now we must accomplish the ultimate clarification</span> = the clarification of eating. <span class="mx">"The earlier ones plowed and sowed and reaped and threshed and ground and sifted — and we have nothing to eat."</span> In the collective, the current clarification is the eating-clarification = the hardest. Desires and evil thoughts flood the mind like literal filth.</div>

<div class="p"><span class="kc">One must strengthen oneself enormously</span> — never falling in spirit. The <span class="h">Baal Davar</span> [Evil One] spreads more than ever before, but correspondingly, Hashem has already sent <span class="h">tzadikim</span> who left behind wondrous books, the likes of which never existed in earlier times. Through them we can hold our ground and draw ourselves to Hashem even amidst such floods. <span class="v">"Were it not that Your Torah is my delight, I would have perished in my affliction."</span></div>

<div class="p">The essential elevation of <span class="h">kavod</span> from the degradation of exile — through <span class="h">ba'alei teshuvah</span> and converts — is accomplished through engaging in Torah <span class="h">lishmah</span> with intention until one arouses the root-souls embedded in Torah. All this is done by the <span class="h">tzadikim</span> who engage in Torah and transmit it to us, drawing illumination from the root-souls within Torah — enabling each person's soul to shine, so he can endure the suffering and strip the soiled garments. The Torah purifies from all impurities: <span class="mx">"as streams flowing — just as streams raise a person from impurity to purity, so words of Torah…"</span></div>

<!-- §8 -->
<div class="sn">§ 8 — <i>Mashiach</i>/Dovid: The Final Clarification</div>

<div class="p">The ultimate completion of this final clarification is accomplished only through <span class="h">Mashiach</span> = Dovid. Dovid prayed: <span class="v">"the humble shall eat and be satisfied — they shall praise Hashem."</span> = the eating-clarification in its perfection. <span class="v">"You prepare a table before me in the presence of my enemies."</span></div>

<div class="p">The Sages: <span class="mx">"In the future, Hashem will make a feast for the <span class="h">tzadikim</span>. When they wish to say <span class="h">Birchas HaMazon</span>, Dovid will say: 'It is fitting for me to bless.'"</span> The ultimate reward = the eating-clarification perfected. Dovid will bless because he accomplishes this. <span class="h">Birchas HaMazon</span> is blessed after eating — after the good has already been extracted. One must mention <span class="h">Eretz Yisrael</span>: <span class="v">"you shall eat, be satisfied, and bless Hashem for the Land."</span> The holiness of <span class="h">Eretz Yisrael</span> is especially in eating-holiness (= <span class="h">mitzvos</span> dependent on the Land: <span class="h">challah</span>, <span class="h">bikkurim</span>, <span class="h">terumos</span>, <span class="h">ma'asros</span>, sacrifices). Dovid conquered <span class="h">Eretz Yisrael</span> fully because conquering = the final eating-clarification = <span class="h">mitzvos</span> of eating dependent on the Land.</div>

<!-- §9 -->
<div class="sn">§ 9 — <i>Shabbos</i>: Eating Without Soiled Garments — <i>Lechem Mishneh</i></div>

<div class="p"><span class="h">Shabbos</span> = a taste of the World to Come, when eating is a great <span class="h">mitzvah</span>. On <span class="h">Shabbos</span>, <span class="v">"all evildoers scatter"</span> — the <span class="h">Sitra Achra</span> (= soiled garments) has no grip on <span class="h">Shabbos</span> food. <span class="h">Shabbos</span> eating is entirely holy. Bathing before <span class="h">Shabbos</span> = washing away soiled garments: <span class="v">"when Hashem has washed the filth of the daughters of Tziyon."</span> Then donning <span class="h">Shabbos</span> clothes = clean garments, white garments.</div>

<div class="p"><span class="kc"><span class="h">Lechem Mishneh</span></span> [two whole loaves]: On weekdays, even when one blesses on a whole loaf, one immediately breaks it — because the eating-clarification begins, and the previous completeness is temporarily nullified (the loaf is now <span class="h">palga</span>). There is effort and delay before the new completeness emerges. But on <span class="h">Shabbos</span> — no clarification is needed! All sparks extracted during the six weekdays rise to their rest immediately. Therefore: <span class="kc">even when one breaks the bread, the second whole loaf remains on the table</span> — completeness is never absent from the table, not even for a moment. The life-force in the bread rises instantly to its ultimate completeness without effort. This is <span class="h">Lechem Mishneh</span>: even during the breaking, the whole remains. On weekdays, completeness temporarily disappears from the table; on <span class="h">Shabbos</span>, never.</div>

<!-- §10 -->
<div class="sn">§ 10 — <i>Shabbos</i>: Torah, <i>Teshuvah</i>, Humility, and Peace</div>

<div class="p">All the <span class="h">tikkunim</span> that draw <span class="h">shalom</span> (described in <span class="lr">LM I:14</span>) are accomplished on <span class="h">Shabbos</span>: Torah was given on <span class="h">Shabbos</span>; reading Torah publicly in the scroll from <span class="h">Bereishis</span> to <span class="v">"before the eyes of all Israel"</span> every <span class="h">Shabbos</span> — with <span class="h">te'amim</span> and <span class="h">nekudos</span> — arouses the root-souls. <span class="h">Shabbos</span> = <span class="h">teshuvah</span>: <span class="v">"you shall return [<span class="h">v'shavta</span>] to Hashem your G-d."</span> The essential <span class="h">zivug</span> [union] is on <span class="h">Shabbos</span>, drawing a pure soul. On <span class="h">Shabbos</span>, <span class="h">kavod</span> rises through <span class="h">ba'alei teshuvah</span> and converts: <span class="v">"ascribe to Hashem, families of nations — ascribe to Hashem glory and strength!"</span> (recited at <span class="h">Kabbalas Shabbos</span>). <span class="h">Yirah</span> is completed = <span class="h">Shabbos Shalom</span>. All through humility: <span class="v">"let each man remain in his place"</span> — seeing one's own lowliness.</div>

<!-- §11 -->
<div class="sn">§ 11 — Rus: Conversion Through the Clarification of Eating</div>

<div class="p"><span class="v">"Wash yourself, anoint yourself, and put on your garments"</span> — <span class="mx">"these are <span class="h">Shabbos</span> garments."</span> Rus was a true convert who achieved a wondrous <span class="h">tikkun</span>, meriting that <span class="h">Mashiach</span> descend from her — through whom the ultimate <span class="h">tikkun</span> of eating is completed: banishing soiled garments entirely and meriting clean garments = <span class="h">Shabbos</span> garments. <span class="v">"Spread your wings over your maidservant"</span> = wings of <span class="h">mitzvah</span> = <span class="h">tzitzis</span> = white garments = wings of the <span class="h">Shechinah</span> under which converts enter.</div>

<div class="p">All of Rus's story occurs during the harvest-clarification and during eating. She entered at the beginning of the harvest and stayed through its end. Her essential <span class="h">tikkun</span> was at the time of eating: <span class="v">"Boaz said to Rus: at mealtime, come here"</span> — <span class="mx">"at mealtime"</span> precisely! As a convert emerging from impurity, she needed the eating-clarification. <span class="v">"She ate, was satisfied, and had left over"</span> — <span class="mx">"she ate = in this world; was satisfied = in the days of <span class="h">Mashiach</span>; had left over = in the World to Come."</span> She merited that her eating embodied the clarification <span class="h">Mashiach</span> (her descendant) will accomplish — the ultimate eating-clarification without any grip of soiled garments.</div>

<div class="p">She merited to bear Oved = <span class="v">"he who works his land shall be satisfied with bread."</span> Oved, from whom <span class="h">Mashiach</span> descends, is the aspect of the clarifications of grain and bread. The essential <span class="h">tikkun</span> specifically comes through one who was very far, who then awakens to come to the true <span class="h">tzadikim</span> — for this is the ultimate <span class="h">kavod</span>: when the most distant and soiled awaken to Hashem.</div>

<!-- §12 -->
<div class="sn">§ 12 — Words of Torah at the Table</div>

<div class="p">Therefore one must say words of Torah at the table — for the essential clarification from soiled garments is through Torah, which arouses the root-souls. <span class="mx">"Any table at which words of Torah are not spoken — it is as if they ate from the sacrifices of the dead."</span> As it is said: <span class="v">"all their tables are full of vomit and filth, without the Place [<span class="h">Makom</span>]."</span> Without Torah, the soiled garments cannot be purified. "Sacrifices of the dead" = idolatry = the side of death that came through Adam's eating-sin.</div>

<!-- §13 -->
<div class="sn">§ 13 — <i>Chanukah</i>: Purification Through Torah-Light</div>

<div class="p"><span class="h">Chanukah</span> = inauguration of the <span class="h">Bais HaMikdash</span>. The holiness of <span class="h">batei knesses</span> and <span class="h">batei midrash</span> — where Torah and prayer are engaged — are the aspect of <span class="h">mikdash me'at</span> [miniature sanctuary]. The essential purification from soiled garments is through the holy Torah = <span class="mx">"a well of living waters," "a river that does not cease," "a spring that ever strengthens."</span></div>

<div class="p">The soiled garments of anyone who has not rectified his sins — especially one who has become very soiled — cause obstacles and barriers to intensify constantly, spreading before him like a river, clinging like <span class="v">"miry clay with no foothold"</span> (<span class="h">y'ven metzulah</span>). From this come all the falls and estrangement. Even when one is stirred to return, the barriers immediately spread. Dovid cried: <span class="v">"I have sunk in miry depths with no foothold… rescue me from the mud, let me not drown."</span></div>

<div class="p">The remedy = the holy Torah, the source of living waters that purifies from all filth: <span class="v">"I will sprinkle upon you pure waters and you shall be purified from all your impurities."</span> <span class="kc">Every person must know: even if he is whatever he is — if he flees to Torah every day, whether much or little, establishing fixed times and never missing a day — he will certainly in the end be purified from all soiled garments and draw close to Hashem in truth.</span> Though the soiled garments are deeply entangled and intensify every time one is aroused toward Hashem — especially when engaging in Torah and prayer — nonetheless, if one does not abandon his place, forcing his thought into Torah and prayer each time, the Torah will certainly purify him. For <span class="kc">the Torah is a river that does not cease and a spring that ever strengthens</span> — no impurity can stand against it.</div>

<div class="p"><span class="h">Chanukah</span>: the forces of <span class="h">Malchus Yavan HaResha'ah</span> [the wicked Greek kingdom] represent <span class="h">y'ven metzulah</span> — the miry depths of all obstacles from soiled garments. Their goal: <span class="mx">"write on the horn of the ox that you have no share in the G-d of Israel!"</span> They seek to sever one completely. The victory came through the <span class="h">Kohanim</span> = the true <span class="h">tzadikim</span> who engage in Torah constantly, arousing the root-souls. Through the Torah's living waters, they purify from the soiled garments and merit white garments.</div>

<div class="ts">
  <div class="sh">✦ Translator's Summary — Part 1 (§§ 1–13) ✦</div>

  <p style="margin-bottom:.8rem;">This halachah — based on <strong>LM I:14</strong> — develops a grand metaphor: <strong>the clarification of bread from raw wheat to digested food = the soul's journey from impurity to ultimate purity.</strong> Every stage of bread production (plowing, reaping, threshing, grinding, sifting, kneading, baking) = a stage of spiritual clarification. But the <strong>ultimate clarification happens during eating itself</strong> — inside the body, where the finest good rises as life-force and the waste becomes literal refuse. This = the final battle with the <em>begadim tzo'im</em> (soiled garments) = the <em>kelippos</em> clinging to the soul.</p>

  <p style="margin-bottom:.8rem;"><strong>Key teachings</strong>: The whole loaf = <em>yirah</em> = completeness. Breaking = beginning the ultimate clarification. Rav Ashi/Menasheh: the secret of <em>HaMotzi</em> is the secret of purification from idolatry's filth. <em>Shabbos</em> = eating without soiled garments; <em>Lechem Mishneh</em> = completeness never absent from the table. Rus = the convert whose eating-<em>tikkun</em> birthed <em>Mashiach</em>. <em>Chanukah</em> = Torah-light that overcomes the "miry depths" of obstacles.</p>

  <p style="font-style:italic;">Part 2 continues with §§14-23 (Chanukah oil, midnight study, strengthening oneself) and Part 3 with §§24-45+ (Menorah, graves of tzadikim, fixed place for meals, Yosef, and the angels' meal).</p>
